# Direction C (plan v2.9 Sec. 3 C): the race on each rung of the linear-to-ViT ladder — working note

Owner: `experiment-repo`. Status: development (2026-10-18); rungs 1a, 1b, 2, 4 and 5. Labels as in `theory/derivations.md`.

## Rung 1a. Deep linear encoder (code `code/e6_r1.py`; tolerance `results/e6/r1_tolerance.json`)
Encoder $W=W_L\cdots W_1$ with weight decay $\lambda$ on every factor and on the predictor, identity predictor
$P_0=p_0I$, copy target, gradient descent. With aligned statistics and aligned factors, direction k has factor
amplitudes $w_1,\dots,w_L$, end-to-end amplitude $a=\prod_iw_i$ and predictor gain $p$; with $h(p)=pc-p^2\sigma$,
$$\dot w_i=h(p)\,a^2/w_i-\lambda w_i,\qquad \dot p=\alpha[(c-p\sigma)a^2-\lambda p].$$

**Balance across layers (exact; Known in substance: layer balancedness of deep linear nets, here with decay).**
$\tfrac{d}{dt}(w_i^2-w_j^2)=-2\lambda(w_i^2-w_j^2)$ and, for $\alpha=1$, $\tfrac{d}{dt}(p^2-w_i^2)=-2\lambda(p^2-w_i^2)$.
So from balanced factors $w_i=a^{1/L}$ and
$$\dot a=L\,[h(p)\,a^{3-2/L}-\lambda a],\qquad p=\sqrt{a^{2/L}+e^{-2\lambda t}(p_0^2-a_0^{2/L})}$$
is exact (the depth-L analogue of Cor. 3a; `race_deep_1d`).

**Near collapse the drive is of higher order (derived).** For $L\ge2$, $d\log a/dt=L[h(p)a^{2-2/L}-\lambda]\to-L\lambda$
as $a\to0$: the collapsed point attracts every direction, whatever the predictor (the origin is a local minimum
under weight decay, `ziyin2022exact`). A direction escapes only from above a basin boundary
$a_b(p)=(\lambda/h(p))^{L/(2L-2)}$.

**Closed-form race (approximation A1, Bernoulli).** With $p_r=p_0e^{-\alpha\lambda t}$ and $u=a^m$, $m=2-2/L$:
$\dot u=mL\,h\,u^2-mL\lambda u$, so $v=1/u$ obeys the linear equation $\dot v=mL\lambda v-mLh$ and
$$v(t)=e^{(2L-2)\lambda t}\Big[a_0^{-m}-(2L-2)\int_0^th(p_r(\tau))\,e^{-(2L-2)\lambda\tau}d\tau\Big].$$
The direction escapes (finite-time blow-up of $u$) iff $a_0^{-m}\le(2L-2)\max_tI(t)$; with the integral to infinity,
$I_\infty=\frac{p_0}{\lambda}\big[\frac{c}{\alpha+2L-2}-\frac{\sigma p_0}{2\alpha+2L-2}\big]$. So survival depends on
the initial amplitude as a power law, $a_0\ge[(2L-2)I^*]^{-1/m}$: the basin and power-law dependence the plan
anticipated. **Static bound at depth L:** the balanced fixed point needs $\lambda=p^{2L-1}(c-p\sigma)$, so it exists
iff $\lambda\le\lambda^*_L=\max_pp^{2L-1}(c-p\sigma)$ (for $L=1$, $c^2/(4\sigma)$). The registered prediction counts
directions that escape and have $\lambda\le\lambda^*_L$.

**Where A1 is expected to be weak.** A1 drops $a^{2/L}$ next to $p_0^2$ in $p$; for $L=3$ and $a_0$ near threshold
($a_0^{2/3}\approx0.5$ at $p_0=1$) that is not small, and exploration put the exact threshold below A1's. The
development grid measures this; the exact reduction is reported beside it.

## Rung 1b. Deep predictor with random factors (L6; code `code/e6_r1b.py`; tolerance `results/e6/r1b_tolerance.json`)
Depth-1 encoder $W$, predictor $P=Q_M\cdots Q_1$ with arbitrary (Gaussian or perturbed-identity) factors, decay
$\lambda$ on $W$ and on every factor, copy target, gradient descent. The delivered capture correction for depth-$M$
predictors (E1c) was derived for identity factors $qI$, where $p=q^M$ and $\dot p=\alpha M[p^{2-2/M}(c-p\sigma)a^2-\lambda p]$.

**Capture along a latent direction for general factors (derived).** Near capture the predictor's gradient is the
rank-one $-(c-p_u\sigma)a^2uu^\top$ along the latent direction $u$ of the growing mode. Each factor moves by
$Q_{>j}^\top(\cdot)Q_{<j}^\top$ ($Q_{>j}=Q_M\cdots Q_{j+1}$, $Q_{<j}=Q_{j-1}\cdots Q_1$), so $p_u=u^\top Pu$ obeys
$$\dot p_u=\alpha\big[(c-p_u\sigma)a^2K_u-M\lambda p_u\big],\qquad K_u=\sum_{j=1}^M|Q_{>j}^\top u|^2\,|Q_{<j}u|^2,$$
and the mode captures the predictor once $a^2(c-p_u\sigma)K_u\ge M\lambda p_u$. For identity factors $K_u=Mp^{2-2/M}$ and
this is E1c's rule $a^2\ge\lambda p^{2/M-1}/(c-p\sigma)$; for $M=1$, $K_u=1$ and it is the delivered depth-1 rule. Before
capture each factor only decays, $Q_j(t)=Q_j(0)e^{-\alpha\lambda t}$, so $K_u(t)=e^{-2(M-1)\alpha\lambda t}K_u(0)$.
The criterion `n1g_K` integrates the delivered N1-general linear system (the actual $W_0$ and $P_0$, the predictor
decaying as $e^{-M\alpha\lambda t}$) and applies this rule to each singular triplet of the linear $W$.

**Development** (`results/e6/r1b_dev/`, 216 runs, 40 s; `python3 code/e6_r1b.py run dev results/e6/r1b_dev 8`).
Final-count MAE pooled 0.47 (within-1 0.96); on $M\ge2$: Gaussian factors 0.40, perturbed identity 0.38, within-1 1.00
in both. The delivered depth-1 rule (`n1g_d1`) has 0.69 overall; its paired CI against `n1g_K` lies above 0 pooled over
$M\ge2$ in each arm, but includes 0 at $M=2$ alone (where $p^{2/M-1}=1$ and the rules differ least). E1c's rule applied
with the effective $p_i$ (`n1g_pL`) is indistinguishable from `n1g_K` on development (CI of the difference [−0.01,
0.05]). The identity-factor race with the nominal gain (`race_id`, 1.89), the static bound (8.84), the initial window
with the actual $P_0$ (6.85) and every $\mu$ variant (best `dvd_pq` 2.12; `dvd_exact` 3.86) are far behind; so is the
count at $t=25$ or 50. The count read at 10% of $T$ matches `n1g_K` on Gaussian factors (CI [−0.16, 0.17]) and is
beaten on perturbed identity ([0.08, 0.46]).

**Held-out (registered, scored once; `results/e6/r1b_heldout/r1b_heldout_score.json`).** Both verdict cells pass. On
$M\ge2$, `n1g_K` has MAE 0.39 (Gaussian factors) and 0.23 (perturbed identity), within-1 0.96 / 0.99, and beats every
registered baseline, with paired CIs above 0: the delivered depth-1 rule, the identity race, the static bound, the
initial window, the early counts and all $\mu$ variants. Two qualifications:
- On Gaussian factors the gain over the depth-1 rule resolves only when depths are pooled; at single $M$ it does not.
- The count read at 10% of $T$ is better there than `n1g_K` (0.24 vs 0.39).
So the $K_u$ correction is supported as the right capture rule for random predictor factors. On Gaussian factors, though,
an early reading of the run is as good or better as an operational predictor.

## Rung 2. MLP encoder under gradient descent (code `code/e6_r2.py`, scoring `code/e6_r23_a2.py`; registration `results/e6/prediction_r23.json`)
Encoder $f(x)=W_2\,\phi(W_1x)$ with $\phi=\tanh$ or ReLU (no biases), hidden width $H$, predictor $P$ ($P_0=p_0I$),
copy target with stop-gradient, weight decay $\lambda$ on $W_1$, $W_2$ and $P$, full-batch GD with step $\eta$ on a
fixed sample of $N$ (context, target) pairs. Write $A_c=X_cW_1^\top$, $H_c=\phi(A_c)$, $H_t=\phi(X_tW_1^\top)$,
$Z=HW_2^\top$ and $L=\frac1{2N}\|Z_cP^\top-\mathrm{sg}(Z_t)\|_F^2$.

**Gradients (exact).** With $E=Z_cP^\top-Z_t$ and $G=EP$ (the gradient with respect to $Z_c$),
$$\nabla_PL=\tfrac1NE^\top Z_c,\qquad \nabla_{W_2}L=\tfrac1NG^\top H_c,\qquad
\nabla_{W_1}L=\tfrac1N\big[(GW_2)\odot\phi'(A_c)\big]^\top X_c$$
(`e6_r2.py` `truth`, lines `gP`, `gW2`, `gW1`). The target branch contributes no $W_1$ gradient because of the stop-gradient.

**$W_1$ moves only by its decay before capture (derived; approximation A-C2).** $E$ and $G$ are linear in $W_2$, and
$G$ carries one more factor $P$: $|E|=O((|P|+1)|W_2||H|)$, $|G|=O(|P|(|P|+1)|W_2||H|)$. The $W_1$ gradient multiplies
$G$ by $W_2$ once more, so
$$|\nabla_{W_1}L|\le K\,|P|(|P|+1)\,|W_2|^2\,|W_1|,$$
with $K$ set by the data's fourth moments and $\sup|\phi'|\le1$, using $|H|\le|A|$ (exact homogeneity for ReLU,
$|\tanh a|\le|a|$). The gradient is therefore second order in $|W_2|$, against the first-order decay $\lambda W_1$. (The
code's docstring and an earlier draft of the paper's appendix say "$O(|W_2|)$". That bound holds but is weaker; the
order is $|W_2|^2$.) Write $W_1(t)=d^{\,t}(W_1(0)+D_t)$ with $d=1-\eta\lambda$. Then
$D_{t+1}=D_t-\eta d^{-t-1}\nabla_{W_1}L(t)$, and the relative departure from the decay path obeys
$$\delta(t)=\frac{|W_1(t)-d^{\,t}W_1(0)|}{|d^{\,t}W_1(0)|}\;\lesssim\;\frac{\eta K}{d}\sum_{s<t}|P_s|(|P_s|+1)|W_2(s)|^2 .$$
While every direction of $W_2$ is near its initial size $w_0=10^{-3}$, the sum is $O(p_0(p_0+1)w_0^2\,t)$ and $\delta$
stays far below 1. Once a direction escapes ($|W_2|$ of order the rank threshold or larger), the bound is no longer
small. From then on $W_1$ learns, and the decay path is an approximation. The race uses the statistics up to the step
at which its own count is decided. That step is the first escape for the first direction, and later for the others.

**The statistics the race integrates.** Along the decay path the hidden statistics are $S_{11}(t)=\frac1NH_c^\top H_c$
and $S_{21}(t)=\frac1NH_t^\top H_c$ at $W_1=d^{\,t}W_1(0)$.
- For ReLU (degree-1 homogeneous), $S(t)=d^{\,2t}S(0)$ exactly.
- For tanh they are recomputed on the sample.

In both cases `n1g_tv` refreshes them every STAT_EVERY = 50 steps and holds them in between. So, within the criterion
as registered, the statistics lag the path by up to 49 steps. For ReLU that is a relative excess of at most
$1-d^{98}$: 3.4% / 6.6% / 12.8% at λ = 0.0035 / 0.007 / 0.014 with η = 0.1. For tanh it is about the same or less,
since tanh's statistics shrink no faster than $d^{2t}$. This hold error is part of the registered criterion, not of the theory.
`r_decay` integrates the near-collapse linear system of N1-general (approximation A1) with these statistics:
$\dot W_2=P^\top W_2S_{21}-P^\top PW_2S_{11}-\lambda W_2$, with $P(t)=d^{\,t}P_0$. It counts directions per check as
N1-general does, and the prediction is the maximum count over checks. `r_init` is the same integration with $S(0)$
frozen, i.e. the delivered E2 MLP predictor, which ignores the $W_1$ decay.

**Numerical check on the delivered held-out runs** (post hoc, `code/checks/e6_r2_w1path.py` →
`results/e6/r2_heldout/posthoc/w1path.json`). The check re-trains the 144 base runs of `r2_heldout` with the frozen
operations in the same order, recording every 50 steps:
- $\delta(t)$;
- $|\nabla_{W_1}L|/(|P||W_2|^2|W_1|)$;
- the relative error of $S_{11}$ and $S_{21}$ at the run's own $W_1(t)$ against the decay path;
- the $W_2$ singular values.

It also records, from an instrumented copy of `n1g_tv`, the step at which `r_decay`'s count is decided.
Results (142 finite runs of 144; runs 399 and 423 diverge, as delivered):
- **Reproduction.** The re-trained final embedding rank equals the delivered `truth.emb_rank`, and the instrumented
  `r_decay` equals the delivered `pred.r_decay`, on all 142 finite runs.
- **Gradient order.** Before the first escape (120 runs with a record before it), $|\nabla_{W_1}L|/(|P||W_2|^2|W_1|)$
  stays bounded: median 0.11, q90 0.20, max 0.35 (tanh max 0.35, ReLU max 0.15). This is consistent with the
  $O(|P|(|P|+1)|W_2|^2)$ bound; the constant does not grow with $|W_2|$.
- **Up to the first escape the decay path holds.** Before the first escape the maximum $\delta$ has median
  $3.4\times10^{-5}$, q90 $3.9\times10^{-4}$, max $4.3\times10^{-3}$. The statistic error has median $1.5\times10^{-4}$,
  q90 $2.1\times10^{-3}$, max 0.031. At the first escape (123 runs), $\delta$ has median $7.3\times10^{-4}$, max 0.021.
- **After it, the path breaks down.** At the last escape (the record at which $W_2$ first reaches its final rank),
  $\delta$ has median 0.080, q90 0.41, max 12; the statistic error has median 0.098, q90 1.9. By the end of training
  $\delta$ is $O(10^2$–$10^3)$ in every run with truth ≥ 1. With truth 0 it is median $3\times10^{-5}$, so there
  $W_1$ only decays.
- **Where the race decides.** `r_decay`'s count reaches its maximum at a median of 9% of $T$ (q90 18%, max 24%).
  That step comes before the first escape in 51 of 142 runs (32 escaped runs plus the 19 that never escape). In the
  other 91 runs the race integrates decay-path statistics past the point where $W_1$ has started to learn. At that
  step the statistic error has median 0.032, q90 1.2, max 11.3.
  - By cell (median / q90): field-ReLU 0.004 / 0.14, field-tanh 0.060 / 1.24, latent-ReLU 0.048 / 0.25,
    latent-tanh 0.49 / 2.9.
- **The departure tracks `r_decay`'s error** (association, not a registered test). Split by terciles of the statistic
  error at the decision step, `r_decay`'s MAE is 0.09 / 0.40 / 0.75; split by whether the decision comes before the
  first escape, it is 0.18 (51 runs) vs 0.55 (91 runs). The cell with the largest departure, latent-tanh, also has
  the largest MAE (0.92, against 0.17 / 0.25 / 0.32 for field-tanh / field-ReLU / latent-ReLU).

**Label.**
- The decay path is exact to $O(\eta K p_0(p_0+1)w_0^2t)$ before capture, and validated numerically up to the first
  escape ($\delta\le4\times10^{-3}$ in every run).
- After the first escape it is an approximation. It is validated where the race decides early (ReLU, field data) and
  located as failing in the latent-tanh cell. There most runs decide after $W_1$ has moved by tens of percent in the
  statistics.

The residual error of `r_decay` on rung 2 is therefore located in the later directions' window. In that window
$W_1$ learns, and a criterion that follows $W_1$'s learned statistics (not its decay) would be needed. That is the
mechanism rung 4 tests under the LN target, where capture is immediate.

Per-run records (every 50 steps: $\delta$, gradient ratio, statistic errors, $W_2$ singular values) are in
`results/e6/r2_heldout/posthoc/w1path_records.jsonl.gz`; per-run summaries are in `w1path.json` `rows`. Cost: 617 s
on 8 processes.

## Rung 4. MLP encoder with a layer-normalised target, GD (code `code/e6_r4.py`, held-out `code/e6_r4_heldout.py`; registration `results/e6/prediction_r4.json`)

**Model.** Rung 2's encoder and predictor, with A4's target:
$L=\frac1{2N}\sum|PW_2\phi(W_1x_c)-\mathrm{sg}\,\mathrm{LN}(W_2\phi(W_1x_t))|^2+\frac\lambda2(|W_1|^2+|W_2|^2+|P|^2)$, with
$\mathrm{LN}(y)=\Pi y/\sqrt{|\Pi y|^2/d+\epsilon}$. Truth: the embedding rank of $\Pi Z$ at $T$.

**Why rung 2's reduction does not carry over (order-of-magnitude argument, not a theorem).**
- In rung 2 the residual $E=Pz_c-z_t$ is $O(|W_2|)$, so $\nabla_{W_1}=O(|P||W_2|^2)$. Near collapse ($w_0=10^{-3}$) this is negligible against $\lambda W_1$, and $W_1$ follows $(1-\eta\lambda)^tW_1(0)$ until the first escape (rung 2 check: $\delta\le4\times10^{-3}$).
- Under the LN target the residual does not shrink with $|W_2|$, in two phases (A4's N2-ii.2 with its $\epsilon$ qualification, review A4-T1).
  - *$\epsilon$-linear phase.* At $t=0$ the held-out targets have $|\Pi z_t|^2/d=0.014$–$0.049\,\epsilon$, median $0.027\,\epsilon$ (`results/e6/r4_eps_regime.json`, `code/checks/e6_r4_eps_regime.py`; initial weights only). So $\mathrm{LN}(z_t)\approx\Pi z_t/\sqrt\epsilon$: a gain of about 312 ($1/\sqrt\epsilon=316$) at an rms scale of about 0.16. The target is still linear in $|W_2|$ here, but it is about 300 times the plain target. $E\approx-\Pi z_t/\sqrt\epsilon$, and $\nabla_{W_1}=O(|P||W_2|^2/\sqrt\epsilon)$.
  - *Scale-free phase.* Once $|W_2|$ has grown by 4.5–8.5 times ($|\Pi z_t|^2/d=\epsilon$; within about 5% of scale-free at 14–27 times), the target has unit scale whatever $|W_2|$ is. Then $E\approx-\mathrm{LN}(z_t)=O(1)$ while $z_c$ is small, and $\nabla_{W_1}=O(|P||W_2|)$, one power of $|W_2|$ fewer than in rung 2.
  - In both phases the drive on $W_1$ is far larger than rung 2's at the same $|W_2|$: by the factor $1/\sqrt\epsilon$ in the first, and by $1/|W_2|$ in the second.
- The same drive grows $W_2$ at once (N2-ii: rank $\ge1$, no escape window). Within a fraction of the first e-fold, $|\nabla_{W_1}|$ is then comparable to $\lambda|W_1|$, and the first layer learns from the start.
- So `n2_decay`, A4's flow on rung 2's decay-path statistics, has no regime in which its premise holds, and its error is not a small correction. Its error does not measure the first layer's learning either. The dev diagnostic (`results/e6/r4_dev/posthoc/diag.json`) gives about the same miss whatever the flow is told about W1: MAE 8.08 with W1 frozen (`n2_init`), 8.40 with W1 measured (`n2_meas_full`) and 8.43 on the decay path (`n2_decay`).

**Development (`results/e6/r4_dev`, results README "rung 4").**
- $\delta$ at the first check is 0.39–4.1 on every finite run (median 0.84), and $|W_1(T)|$ is about $|W_1(0)|$ rather than $e^{-8}|W_1(0)|$.
- The tanh rank rises over training. `n2_decay` under-counts it by 12.8 on average (MAE 8.4 over all finite runs).
- The post hoc diagnostic feeds the same flow the measured $W_1(t)$ (`n2_meas_full`; in a fixed or rotating basis). It still under-counts by about 8, so the failure is not only the first layer's statistics.
- Two parts of the reduced flow are approximations in this model: the per-direction gain $G$ (A4's N2-i is exact for Gaussian targets; $W_2\phi(W_1x_t)$ is not Gaussian), and the diagonal, one-amplitude-per-direction coupling (exact for A4's orthogonal init, N2-iii). Which of them breaks is not tested.

**Registered for the held-out grid** (`results/e6/prediction_r4.json`):
- the criterion `n2_decay` (A4 passed, so the plan's exit names it), with rung 2's tolerance and "fail" as the stated expectation;
- the located cause as three statements: R4-a (decay-path departure at once), R4-b (under-count on tanh) and R4-c (rank growth after the early check on tanh);
- `n2_meas_full` as a measured conditional row, with dev expectation "does not".

## Rung 5 (E7). The race in the ViT — development (exploratory, 2026-10-18; not registered)
Development data: the delivered E3 force exports (`results/e3/forces.jsonl`, 34 configs x 3 seeds, steps 0-3800 of the
first 20 epochs of a 50-epoch schedule) and E8's 100 instrumented runs (`--instrument 1`: per target-token eigendirection
A, D, S on a fixed probe batch at every log step). Script `code/checks/e7_dev_e3_readouts.py` →
`results/e7/dev/e3_readouts.json`.

- **Readouts from early-training forces do not beat the step-0 rule on E3.** The epoch-20 count MAE over 102 runs is:

  | Readout | MAE | Arm T | Arm P |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | step-0 rule (registered in E3) | 20.9 | 15.7 | 24.1 |
  | forces of step t held over the horizon | 39.7–49.6 | | |
  | same, averaged over (0, t] | 40.7–46.6 | | |
  | count read at t | 23.5 (t = 1600) to 43.0 (t = 200) | | |
  | log-eigenvalue trend t/2..t extrapolated | 18.5 (t = 1600) | 11.0 | 23.1 |

  The trend extrapolation is the only readout better than the step-0 rule, and only from t = 1600 (8 of 50 epochs). It is
  an early reading with a slope, not a race.
- **The linear model's sign structure fails in the ViT after the first steps.** In the linear model the drive part of a
  direction's growth is positive (2 e p c) and the self-prediction part negative (-2 e p² s), so the static capacity
  c²/(4s) can be read from the two parts without knowing p. Among the counted directions (e_k ≥ 1e-3 max) of Arm T:
  - drive > 0 > self holds in 0.29 of directions at step 0, then 0.07, 0.13, 0.17 and 0.45 at steps 50, 200, 800 and 3200;
  - drive < 0 < self, the reverse, holds in 0.92, 0.85 and 0.75 at steps 50, 200 and 800.
  
  Arm P is similar: 0.57 to 0.69 reversed at steps 50 to 200. So during the window in which the count is decided, the
  cross term of the loss shrinks most counted online directions and the self-prediction term grows them.
  
  The eigenvalues are those of the *centred* online token covariance on unmasked images, while the loss acts through
  the predictor's attention over context and mask tokens. A term can therefore shrink the features' norm while
  spreading their centred covariance. The per-direction capacity readout is undefined on these directions. Not yet
  resolved: whether the reversal comes from centring (mean-token motion), from the predictor's input nonlinearity, or
  from the predictor's effective gain.
- **E8 instrumentation (A, D, S per target direction).** In Arm T at high decay (wd 0.03), the per-step log-growth of a
  direction's variance regresses on its net drive per unit variance (D − S)/A with r = 0.33–0.76 across directions
  within a log interval.
  - The intercept is −4.8e-3 per step, about 3.2 lr·wd during training; at epoch 50 it is −3.0e-3 = 2 lr·wd.
  - In the low → high switch at epoch 5 the intercept moves from about 0 to −4.7e-3 at the switch.
  - At low decay and in Arm P the regression is noise-dominated (|r| < 0.5).
  
  So, at least in Arm T at high decay, a direction's growth separates into a drive-dependent part and a decay part
  proportional to lr·wd.
- **The headroom race** (`code/checks/e7_dev_headroom.py` → `results/e7/dev/headroom_race.json`; post hoc on delivered
  runs, development). Weight decay is decoupled in `code/vit_jepa.py` (p ← p(1 − lr·wd) per step, SGD and AdamW alike),
  so a decayed linear output map loses exactly 2 lr(t) wd(t) per step in log variance; the E8 intercept above
  (2-3.3 lr·wd) is that rate. The per-direction drive coefficient, by contrast, is not stable (its slope on (D − S)/A
  falls from 0.5 lr to 0.004 lr per step between steps 0-50 and 3900-9750 in Arm T hi). The readout therefore keeps the
  drive only through the spectrum it has built by the readout step t_r and races each direction's log headroom against
  the decay still to come, with no fitted constant:
  alive_k(T) iff log(e_k(t_r)/e_max(t_r)) − 2 Σ_{t_r ≤ t < T} lr(t) wd(t) ≥ log 1e-3, the top direction held.
  - E3 Arm T (39 runs, epoch-20 count): MAE 4.6 at t_r = 200 (4.9 at 400, 5.6 at 800-1560) against 15.7 for the
    registered step-0 rule and 18.9-20.7 for the count read at t_r.
  - The Arm T transition (seed-mean count below half the init count, log-interpolated along wd): measured 0.0070;
    headroom race 0.0068 (t_r = 200) and 0.0070 (t_r = 400); step-0 rule 0.0159 (2.3x off on this interpolated
    definition; 2.6x on the grid definition of E3's C-loc).
  - E8 Arm T (50 runs, 50 epochs, constant and switch rows): MAE 4.0-5.9 against 11.6-17.5 for the count read at t_r. It
    follows the hi → lo switches (e2 20.4 vs 21.6 measured, e5 13.4 vs 13.0, e10 4.0 vs 6.0 at t_r = 800). Its two
    errors are structural: it misses the 4-5 directions that survive any decay (predicts 0-1 at wd 0.03), and it
    under-counts the low-decay row (25.6 vs 39 at wd 1e-3), where the drive keeps adding directions after t_r.
  - **Arm P: it fails** (E3 MAE 46-59, E8 54-66, worse than the count read at t_r). Arm P normalises the target and the
    predictor and does not decay the norm gains, so the output scale is a flat direction and decay does not act on
    the spectrum through it (Sec. "Normalisation and scale-flat directions" of `theory/derivations.md`). The toggles
    from T to P locate which normalisation breaks it.
  - Its prediction for the transition depends on the horizon, which a static (capacity) criterion does not: from the
    low-decay reference spectra at t_r = 200-400, λ_c = 0.0062-0.0081 at 20 epochs and 0.0024-0.0030 at 50 epochs
    (E8 T_lo and E3 T_wd0 / T_wd0.001). No delivered Arm T row lies between wd 1e-3 and 0.03 at 50 epochs, so the
    50-epoch transition is untested.
  - Caveats. t_r and the half-init transition definition were chosen while looking at E3; the decay coefficient 2 is
    fixed by the optimiser, not fitted. The readout uses the spectrum of the run at t_r (1-2% of a 50-epoch run).
- **The μ count of arXiv 2610.02344 in this ViT** (`code/baselines/vit_mu.py`, reimplemented from the definition of
  their image estimator; `code/checks/e7_dev_vit_mu.py` → `results/e7/dev/vit_mu_e3.json`; development, on E3's 99
  runs at fixed wd). Their estimator linearises the encoder at its input tokens (patch + positional embedding) and the
  predictor at proj·embed. Its native count #{μ > 1} runs over width × predictor-width mode pairs: about 1,100 of
  8,192 here, which is not on the scale of a rank count out of 128 (MAE 1,113 in T, 1,101 in P). Pairing the two
  factors index-wise gives a count out of 128, n_μ = 21 in every row except the predictor-init and mask rows (MAE
  14.5 in T, 72 in P). Adding the decoupled decay as the additive floor of their linear criterion
  ((γ − σ)/(1 − momentum) > wd, defined for SGD only) gives MAE 8.7 in T: between the step-0 rule (15.7) and the
  headroom race (4.6). It moves too little with decay: from 21 at wd 0 to 9 at wd 0.1, against 51 to 4 measured. It
  has no horizon and does not reach half the init count anywhere on the T_wd row, so it does not place a transition.
- **Which μ reading is theirs (E7 amendment 2).** Everything above is computed at initialisation from the input
  tokens and proj·embed: it is the paper's *tabular* estimator transferred to the ViT, and their App. K.1 reports that
  this init-time reading is null on images. Their own count is r̃_tight (Cor. 3.3; App. A.4 pairs γ and σ of the same
  (k, l)), and their image reading is App. K.2: a signed, target-aligned drive along data eigenmodes after K = 10
  predictor-only steps, with the predictor block's input Jacobian in place of W_p. Amendment 2 registers, beside the
  index-paired variants above (which are my pairings): r̃_tight with Prop. J.1's decay floor in this run's units,
  (2/d)·g·(γ − σ) > wd (`vit_jepa.py` trains on a mean-over-width MSE, whose gradient is 2/d times that of their
  ½E‖·‖²; g = 1/(1 − momentum)), as the μ baseline of H2 with its own λ_c; and a K.2-style reading (`k2_reading`):
  10 predictor-only steps with the run's optimiser, learning rate and decay, the encoder fixed at initialisation as
  its own teacher, W_eff by finite differences over 256 masked training images, γ_k = (1 − p) c_k b_kᵀW_eff b_k and
  σ_k = a_k‖W_eff b_k‖² along each eigenvector b_k of Σ_XX, counted with the same run-unit floor (SGD) or
  #{γ_k > σ_k} (AdamW). It is a reimplementation from their description, not their code. Weights at t_r are not
  saved by the frozen run code, so a K.2 reading at t_r is not available. On the six 3-epoch pilot configurations
  (seed 900, not part of any statistic) r̃_tight = 33, with the run-unit floor 9 (SGD) and 33 (AdamW); the K.2 count is
  0 under SGD at wd 0.0034 (59-61 of 128 modes have positive drive, none clears the floor) and 11 under AdamW.
